TL;DR:
Pros:
-Really beautiful concept art;
-Interesting puzzles;
-Swift and dynamic gameplay;
-Good OST.
Cons:
-Badly programmed (many bugs and performance issues);
- Unconvincing plot;
-Realy bad save mechanics.
The game is really beautiful: is a fantastic aesthetic experience to play it. The gameplay is exciting and keeps you away from boredoom. Exploration and achivement hunting are pleasant experiences. If you enjoy a challenging platformer game, it is definitely worth a try. That being said, the game possesses some big problems which seriously can upset you: first, it is badly programmed. I got higher specs than the reccommended and I got constant lags and sound issues. That is not a problem specifically from my computer, since you can find several such complains in the game forum. A possible solution, if you find yourself experiencing such problems, is to turn V-Sync on and set the game to the lowest resolution - after the game gain some speed, keeping V-Sync turned on, switch back to your prefered resolution. The plot is also not very nice: the conflict end is very unconvincing, one would expect far greater things by what the game proportion promisses. Finaly, the real problem of this game is the save mechanics...even when you get used to saving content before trying anything stupid, there are many points in the game where save is unavailable, and usually this happens at a very hard part. So the result is that you get condemned to repeat many steps in a particular stage sequence just to try to figure out a last part...that is suppose to make things harder (and in fact it does); but it fails to do so in a fun way. The mechanics also punishes you for every mistake you make, and does not reward any inteligent choices or good skill display.
Overall, I would give the game a 8.5/10. Tottaly worth a try if you enjoy platformer games.

Fallout 3 is a first / third-person action role playing game that was made and published by Bethesda Studios in 2008.
It is the third major sequel to the popular Fallout franchise featuring a whole new gameplay mechanic because, unlike its predecessors, it uses the first person / third-person type of gameplay reminiscent of the TES (The Elder Scrolls) series.
The player takes on the role of a vault dweller in Vault 101. After his father leaves the vault for unknown reasons, the player escapes in search of him and discover the truth about the true purpose of the Vaults.
As he steps out into the wasteland, he discovers that the world was not as dangerous as the other vault dwellers were made to believe. Sure it was still a sh** hole filled with dangerous monsters, raiders, fanatics, and mutants but it was not, as irradiated and inhospitable as he thought it was. This world was fully capable of supporting human life.
Explore the so-called “Capital Wasteland” in search of your father and discover the wonders and the horrors that the nuclear apocalypse has brought.
+ Good graphics for its time (8 years old at the time of this review);
+ Vast open world with a lot of places to explore;
+ Good story however...(see CON #1);
+ Immersive and atmospheric game world;
+ Easily moddable;
+ Good adoption of the TES game mechanics;
+ Solid gameplay;
+ Massive amounts of side quests;
+ Massive amounts of items to collect;
+ Crafting mechanics;
+ Good music, sound effects and voice acting;
+ Lengthy game time;
+ Cheap in price;
+ Good DLC selection; and
+ High replayability.
- Very poor ending (not applicable if you have bought and installed the DLC 'Broken Steel' or the 'Game of the Year Edition' of the game);
- Compatibility issues with later versions of Windows (7 and above);
- Compatibility issues with later CPUs (quad-core above);
- Low resolution textures even on high settings;
- Poor level scaling for enemies which may turn the game boring after max level;
- Poor enemy and companion AI;
- Occasional physics glitches; and
- No iron sights feature for the weapons.
+/- Excellent radio music but limited in selection;
+/- Game's difficulty is not that challenging, even on the highest difficulty;
and a wonderful addition to the Fallout franchise. Fallout 3 delivers a wonderful RPG and first person experience that proves once and for all that solid game play mechanics and world building can stand the test of time.
